// **A conserved quantity that is not money.**
//
// This file is H-S8's falsifier, and it is written to be as unfriendly to the hypothesis as
// possible while still being an honest instance of it. H-S8 says one engine and one language
// serve every workload class on a *general relational core*, with banking a library over it;
// §6.6 records what stands in the way — seven banking forms are keywords in the compiler's
// registry rather than a library over general machinery — and §11.3 states the refutation
// condition: a domain that cannot be expressed without a kernel change falsifies the claim.
//
// So: stock movements between warehouses. Units of a SKU are conserved exactly as money is —
// a transfer moves units out of one place and into another, and the two legs must cancel —
// but nothing here is money. A `unit` is not a currency in any sense a bank would recognise:
// it has no scale, no FX, no interest, and a "balance" is a count of physical things.
//
// **What this file may not do is change anything.** No new keyword, no compiler change, no
// kernel change. If the domain needs one, that is the measurement and the hypothesis is
// refuted; `crates/conservation-suite/tests/inventory.rs` runs it and §9.11 reports which
// happened.
//
// The one place the language shows its origin is `currency`, which is the declaration form
// for a graded unit and is named for the domain it was designed in. Declaring `unit` as a
// currency with scale 0 is using the general machinery — a graded abelian group with a
// per-grade zero-sum rule — under a name from banking. That is exactly the shape §6.6
// predicts: the machinery is general, the vocabulary is not, and the cost of the mismatch is
// a word rather than a feature.

schema warehouse {
    // Two SKUs, each a grade of the conserved quantity. Scale 0: half a widget is not a
    // thing, and a language that forced a scale of 2 here would be wrong in the way §3.2
    // says a `Money` type fixed at two decimals is wrong.
    currency widget { scale: 0 }
    currency sprocket { scale: 0 }

    table locations {
        id: Id<Account> primary key,
        site: Text,
        kind: Text,
    }

    // The movement ledger. Identical in shape to a postings ledger, and the conservation
    // rule is the same rule: every movement balances per SKU.
    ledger movements {
        txn: TxnId,
        place: Id<Account>,
        sku: Currency,
        qty: Money,
        idem: IdemKey window 1_000_000.epochs,
        conserve per (txn, sku);
        retain forever;
    }

    index ix_movements on movements (place, sku) anchor;

    // Stock on hand: the same keyed sum that serves a balance.
    view on_hand = movements.group_by(|m| m.place).sum(|m| m.qty)
        serve { consistency: ledger_consistent, materialize: demand };
}

// A transfer between two locations. The units leave one place and arrive at another, and
// the ledger will not seal the movement unless they cancel.
fn move_stock(from: Id<Account>, to: Id<Account>, qty: Money<widget>)
    -> Result<TxnId, TxnError>
    ! { append, debit<widget>, credit<widget> }
{
    txn idem("move", window: 30.days) {
        post(debit(from, qty)?, credit(to, qty))
    }
}

// Receiving from a supplier: units enter the system from a designated origin, which is the
// inventory equivalent of an issuance account. Conservation still holds — the origin goes
// negative, exactly as an issuance account does — and that is the point: a system with no
// origin account cannot state where stock came from.
fn receive(origin: Id<Account>, into: Id<Account>, qty: Money<sprocket>)
    -> Result<TxnId, TxnError>
    ! { append, debit<sprocket>, credit<sprocket> }
{
    txn idem("receive", window: 30.days) {
        post(debit(origin, qty)?, credit(into, qty))
    }
}

// The negative control, written here rather than in a test: a movement that creates a unit
// out of nothing must not check. `crates/conservation-suite/tests/inventory.rs` compiles the
// commented form below and asserts the refusal, so this comment is a specification rather
// than a note.
//
// fn conjure(into: Id<Account>) -> Result<TxnId, TxnError> ! { append, credit<widget> } {
//     txn idem("conjure", window: 30.days) { post(credit(into, qty)) }
// }

// ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
// MISMATCH-T-22-currency-literal. Recorded, not applied.
//
// **What the falsifier found.** Every quantity above arrives as a *parameter*. It has to.
// A money literal in this domain — `5 widget` — does not lex, and the reason is one line in
// `crates/niles-lang/src/lexer.rs`:
//
//     let looks_like_currency = word.len() == 3
//         && word.bytes().all(|b| b.is_ascii_lowercase())
//         && keywords::lookup(word).is_none();
//
// A currency literal's grade must be exactly three lowercase letters. That is ISO 4217's
// shape, hard-coded into the lexer, and `widget` is six letters. `15000 jpy` lexes and
// `5 widget` does not.
//
// **What that does and does not measure.** The *machinery* is general: the schema declares
// two non-financial grades, the currency-row solver proves both movement functions conserve,
// the ledger seals them, `nilesc run` posts WIDGET legs, and nothing in the compiler or the
// kernel was changed to allow any of it. What is not general is the *notation*: a grade
// whose name is not three letters can be named in a type, a schema, an effect row and an
// argument, but not in a literal.
//
// This is left as a mismatch rather than fixed, because the work order that produced this
// file says the outcome of the falsifier is the measurement and a language change to make a
// hypothesis pass is not a measurement. The one-line widening — accept any lowercase word
// that resolves to a declared currency and is not a keyword — is the obvious repair, and it
// is the kind of change that should be made deliberately, with its own diagnostics for the
// ambiguity it introduces (`5 widgets` where `widgets` is a variable), rather than as a
// side effect of wanting a green cell. §9.11 reports it as the limitation it is.
// ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
